<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Teng</h1><p class="st-meta">1046 BCE – 296 BCE (approx.) · capital Teng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny Ji-clan state in Shandong whose ruler asked Mencius how a small state could survive among giants, a dialogue preserved in the Confucian classics.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> enfeoffed his younger brother Cuo Shu Xiu at Teng, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengzhou">Tengzhou</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shandong">Shandong</a>, as part of the network of royal kinsmen planted across the eastern plain after the conquest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Teng was among the smallest Zhou states, ranked as a marquisate but forced to follow larger neighbours such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-lu">State of Lu</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-song">State of Song</a>. In the late 4th century BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-wen-of-teng">Duke Wen of Teng</a> consulted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> on mourning rites and the well-field system of land allotment; their conversations form a chapter of the Mencius.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Teng was conquered by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-song">State of Song</a> under its last king, Yan, around 296 BCE, and its lands later passed to Qi and Chu. Its memory survives chiefly through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>, and in the name of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tengzhou">Tengzhou</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-teng.jpg" alt="State of Teng" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:States_of_Zhou_Dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1046 BCE – 296 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Teng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Duke Wen of Teng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zhou ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Teng</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-671&amp;focus=state-of-teng" aria-label="State of Teng on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7648 909 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Teng lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7968 1165L7940 1188"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7940 1188h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7968 1165h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7968 1165h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=-671&amp;focus=state-of-teng">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

